EXSECUTOR COMPILER OUTPUT AND RUNTIME EXCEPTION
Version 1.0, 2026-09-09

Additional permission under section 7 of the GNU General Public License,
version 3, as published by the Free Software Foundation.

This exception accompanies the LICENSE file in this repository and forms part
of the terms under which Exsecutor is distributed. Where this exception grants
a permission the GPL would otherwise withhold, this exception controls.

The intent, stated plainly before the terms: **the Exsecutor compiler is free
software and must stay free. What you write in Exsecutor is yours.** Compiling
a program with `exsc` places no licensing obligation on that program, on the
code `exsc` emits from it, or on the resulting binaries -- exactly as compiling
with GCC or generating a parser with Bison does not make your program GPL.


0. DEFINITIONS

"The Compiler" means the Exsecutor compiler `exsc`, together with everything in
this repository used to build, test, or verify it. This includes, without
limitation, `compiler/`, `tools/`, `tests/`, the build configuration, and the
compiler-internal runtime described in section 2 below.

"Compiler Output" means any work produced by running the Compiler on input
supplied by you. This includes the C source the backend emits, generated
assembly, object code, executables, interface (`ego`) files, diagnostics, and
any intermediate representation the Compiler writes out.

"Target Runtime" means those parts of this distribution that are designed to be
incorporated into Compiler Output rather than into the Compiler -- the
memory-management and reference-counting support required by section 6 of the
specification, and any module of the standard library (`norma.*`) that the
Compiler links into, or emits into, a compiled program.

"Your Program" means a work you write in the Exsecutor language, or any work
you compile using the Compiler.


1. GRANT OF EXCEPTION

You have permission to propagate Compiler Output, and to propagate a work
formed by combining Your Program with the Target Runtime, under terms of your
choosing, and you are not required to license such a work under the GNU General
Public License.

This permission applies regardless of how the Target Runtime is combined with
Your Program -- static linking, dynamic linking, source inclusion, or emission
of Target Runtime code into Compiler Output by the Compiler itself.

You do not lose this permission by modifying Your Program, and Your Program
does not become a work based on the Compiler merely by having been compiled
with it.


2. WHAT THIS EXCEPTION DOES *NOT* COVER

This distinction is load-bearing and is stated here so it is not misread later.

The Compiler contains its own internal runtime at `compiler/x86_64/rt/` --
arena allocator, vector, insertion-ordered map, string interner, and syscall
wrappers. That code is **part of the Compiler**. It is statically linked into
the `exsc` binary, it is never emitted into Compiler Output, and it is never
linked into Your Program. It is therefore covered by the GPL with no exception,
and this exception grants nothing in respect of it.

"Target Runtime" and "the compiler-internal runtime" are different things that
share a word. Only the former is excepted.

At the time of writing, no Target Runtime exists yet -- the compiler is under
construction and nothing is emitted into user programs. Section 1 is written
forward so that the licensing position is settled before the code lands, rather
than renegotiated afterwards when contributors have accrued.

This exception also does not permit:

  (a) distributing a modified Compiler under terms other than the GPL. If you
      change the Compiler and distribute it, the GPL applies to your changes;

  (b) using the Target Runtime with a compiler other than the Compiler in order
      to escape the GPL on that other compiler's own terms;

  (c) removing or altering this exception's notices when redistributing.


3. THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS

`vendor/` contains third-party code that is **not** covered by the GPL or by
this exception, and is redistributed under its own terms:

  vendor/fasmg-x86/   flat assembler g (fasmg) x86 macro package
                      Copyright (c) Tomasz Grysztar
                      BSD 3-Clause -- see vendor/fasmg-x86/LICENSE.txt
                      Provenance and hashes: vendor/fasmg-x86/PROVENANCE.md

BSD 3-Clause is GPL-compatible, so the combined work distributes cleanly. That
directory keeps its own license and its own copyright notice, and must not be
relicensed or have its notices stripped.


4. PRECEDENT

This exception follows well-established practice for compilers and code
generators. It is modelled on:

  - the FAUST compiler, whose GPL notice states that code generated by the
    FAUST compiler is not covered by the GPL;
  - the GCC Runtime Library Exception, which is why compiling proprietary
    software with GCC does not make it GPL;
  - the Bison parser exception, which permits distributing generated parsers
    under terms of your choice.

It is deliberately shorter and plainer than the GCC Runtime Library Exception,
which carries machinery for problems this project does not have.


5. NO WARRANTY, AND NOT LEGAL ADVICE

The disclaimer of warranty in sections 15 and 16 of the GPL applies to this
exception as it does to the rest of the distribution.

This text was drafted by reference to the precedents named in section 4. It has
not been reviewed by a lawyer. If the licensing position of this project comes
to matter commercially -- to you or to anyone relying on it -- have counsel read
it before relying on it.


